WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.543
Spots of light often did little in cat visual cortex.

2
00:00:07.483 --> 00:00:09.543
So why would a bar at the right angle do better?

3
00:00:10.617 --> 00:00:15.877
David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel recorded single cells in the visual cortex of

4
00:00:15.877 --> 00:00:16.917
anaesthetized cats.

5
00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:22.767
An electrode just outside one cell counted its spikes, while light was projected

6
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:23.767
onto a screen.

7
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.100
Spots often did little.

8
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.300
A slit or bar of light at the right angle drove some cells hard.

9
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:35.323
For cells that answered small spots, they mapped the field spot by spot.

10
00:00:36.223 --> 00:00:40.583
In the cells they called simple cells, the field split into ON and OFF subregions.

11
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:44.467
The subregions are long and narrow, side by side.

12
00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:48.727
A common layout is a narrow ON strip with an OFF strip on each side.

13
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.080
Picture the field as a grid of squares with made-up values.

14
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.000
Plus and minus are balanced, as they roughly are in many real simple cells.

15
00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:03.113
Which will win: a small spot on the ON strip, or a bar lying along it?

16
00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:10.933
The bar wins by a wide margin.

17
00:01:11.813 --> 00:01:13.633
It lands on plus all along the strip.

18
00:01:14.473 --> 00:01:15.793
The spot collects only a little.

19
00:01:16.783 --> 00:01:20.203
This made-up cell fires only when the sum clears its threshold.

20
00:01:21.123 --> 00:01:22.123
The spot falls short.

21
00:01:23.003 --> 00:01:25.763
That is one reason spots often did little in cortex.

22
00:01:26.883 --> 00:01:32.423
Now turn the bar. It leaves the ON strip and lands on both OFF flanks, and the sum

23
00:01:32.423 --> 00:01:33.423
falls to near zero.

24
00:01:34.466 --> 00:01:36.346
Now light the whole field at once.

25
00:01:37.166 --> 00:01:38.166
What does the cell do?

26
00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.030
Plus and minus cancel, so diffuse light does little.

27
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:50.830
A bar along one OFF strip gives a negative sum.

28
00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.130
This toy cell is already silent.

29
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.330
A cell with spontaneous firing would slow down.

30
00:01:57.333 --> 00:02:00.913
So orientation selectivity is not a separate property.

31
00:02:01.773 --> 00:02:04.933
To a first approximation, it comes from the shape of the field.

32
00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:08.763
Make the strips longer but keep them just as narrow.

33
00:02:09.783 --> 00:02:13.123
Now the sum falls faster as the bar turns, so tuning is sharper.

34
00:02:14.216 --> 00:02:16.416
The threshold sharpens tuning further.

35
00:02:17.376 --> 00:02:20.836
Measured spike tuning is narrower than the field's sum alone predicts.

36
00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:23.710
How might such a field be built?

37
00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:29.610
Hubel and Wiesel suggested geniculate cells with ON centres in a row, all exciting

38
00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.710
one cortical cell.

39
00:02:31.683 --> 00:02:33.943
That cell gets an elongated ON strip.

40
00:02:34.843 --> 00:02:38.963
Their surrounds could add OFF flanks, and OFF-centre cells beside the row could

41
00:02:38.963 --> 00:02:39.963
strengthen them.

42
00:02:40.566 --> 00:02:42.426
They offered this as a suggestion.

43
00:02:43.546 --> 00:02:46.666
Recordings in the 1990s supported the aligned input.

44
00:02:47.546 --> 00:02:49.806
Exactly how the flanks are built is less settled.

45
00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.770
So a bar beats a spot because it collects plus all along the ON strip.

46
00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:56.990
A spot collects only a little.
